+# P2MR leaf version 0xe0 and its tripwire outputs
+
+This build adds a P2MR leaf version, `0xe0`, whose elliptic curve signature checks stop working
+once one of a few designated outputs, the *tripwire outputs*, is missing from the UTXO set (see
+[p2mr.md](p2mr.md) for P2MR). It is an experiment for the Ark-0 test signet: a drill of disabling
+elliptic curve checks inside one leaf version, with a tripwire output whose key the operator
+holds and spends at an announced time.
+
+It follows the tripwire idea (P2XX-T) in Pieter Wuille's mail of 2026-06-25,
+[Giving teeth to expected EC disabling: P2XX(-T)(-ML)](https://groups.google.com/g/bitcoindev/c/aWYtPLVPZ3U),
+with two differences of its own: what the rules constrain is one experimental leaf version, and
+the trigger is the spend of designated outputs, where the mail's trigger is any input that
+successfully executes `<NUMS> OP_CHECKSIG`. Anthony Towns's reply in
+[that thread](https://gnusha.org/pi/bitcoindev/aj9SkwXqdRbuVZxH@erisian.com.au/T/) proposes a
+proof in the coinbase instead, and Sjors Provoost's
+[Sjors/bitcoin#121](https://github.com/Sjors/bitcoin/pull/121) implements one in a node. This
+build is not part of BIP 360 or BIP 361, and it is not proposed for mainnet.
+
+What it shows is narrow: a node with these rules refuses, at the block level, any elliptic curve
+signature in a leaf of version `0xe0` from the block after the one that spends a tripwire output,
+or from the height of the rules if that comes later. It does not
+show that any elliptic curve key is broken, and it cannot tell when one will be. Leaves of version
+`0xc0`, P2TR, witness v0 and legacy scripts are not affected. On a signet with a single block
+signer, it says nothing about how miners or a wider network would adopt such a rule.
+
+## Where leaf version 0xe0 executes
+
+The control block of a leaf of version `0xe0` starts with `0xe1` (P2MR requires the low bit to be
+set). A spend through such a leaf:
+
+- at depth zero (a control block of one byte) succeeds without executing the leaf, at every
+  height, like a P2MR leaf of any version. Wallets must never create such an output;
+- at depth one or more, below the height of the rules (see [Activation](#activation)), succeeds
+  without executing the leaf, as a leaf of an unknown version. Policy refuses such spends (see
+  [Policy](#policy)), and no coins should be sent to such leaves before the height;
+- at depth one or more, from the height on (`SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_TRIPWIRE`), executes the leaf
+  exactly as a leaf of version `0xc0`: BIP 342 rules, the validation weight budget, the
+  `OP_SUCCESSx` scan, the signature message and `OP_CHECKMLDSA44` ([p2mr-mldsa44.md](p2mr-mldsa44.md)).
+
+The tapleaf hash commits to the leaf version, so a signature made for a leaf of version `0xc0`
+is not valid in a leaf of version `0xe0` with the same script, nor the other way round.
+
+## Tripwire outputs
+
+The rules come with a list of outpoints. For a block at or above the height, elliptic curve checks
+in leaves of version `0xe0` are disabled when, in the UTXO set its parent leaves, any of those
+outpoints is not *present*. An outpoint is present when it is unspent **and** was created below
+the height of the rules (`Coin::nHeight`). So an outpoint is missing when:
+
+- it was spent: the block that spends it is still checked with the checks enabled, and they are
+  disabled from the next block on;
+- it never existed, for instance after a reset of the chain: the checks are disabled from the
+  height on. Missing state fails closed;
+- the transaction that created it was moved to the height or above by a deep reorganization.
+
+The checks are enabled again only by a reorganization that undoes the spend (or the move) and
+brings the original output back. The state is the UTXO set itself: undo data restores it in a
+reorganization, and restarts, `-reindex`, `-reindex-chainstate`, pruning and the background
+chainstate of an assumeutxo snapshot need nothing else. Each `ConnectBlock` reads it from the view
+it connects the block to, before applying any transaction, so `TestBlockValidity`, `VerifyDB` and
+the background chainstate each decide on their own UTXO set.
+
+The Ark-0 signet is to have two tripwire outputs, each a P2MR output of two leaves of version
+`0xc0` at depth one, `<P> OP_CHECKSIG` and `OP_RETURN`: one with `P` the NUMS point `H` of BIP 341
+(`XOnlyPubKey::NUMS_H`), which nobody can spend, and the drill output, whose key the operator holds.
+
+## Execution
+
+Once the checks are disabled (`SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_EC_DISABLED`), `OP_CHECKSIG`,
+`OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY` and `OP_CHECKSIGADD` in an executed leaf of version `0xe0` fail the script
+(`P2MR_EC_DISABLED`) when the public key is 32 bytes and the signature is not empty. The script
+fails; no false is pushed, so `OP_NOT` cannot turn the result around. The order of the checks,
+which decides the error reported:
+
+1. `OP_CHECKSIGADD` parses its number first (`SCRIPTNUM`), as before.
+2. A non-empty signature uses up 50 units of the validation weight budget
+   (`TAPSCRIPT_VALIDATION_WEIGHT`), as before.
+3. An empty public key fails (`TAPSCRIPT_EMPTY_PUBKEY`), as before.
+4. A 32-byte public key with a non-empty signature fails (`P2MR_EC_DISABLED`), whatever the
+   signature: valid, invalid, of a wrong size or with a wrong hash type. The signature is never
+   checked, so no signature cache entry can make it pass.
+
+Checks done before the script runs come first: a witness element over 520 bytes fails as
+`PUSH_SIZE`. Not affected: an empty signature (`OP_CHECKSIG` pushes false, `OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY`
+fails, `OP_CHECKSIGADD` adds nothing), public keys of other sizes (they succeed as upgradable key
+types, which policy refuses), and `OP_CHECKMLDSA44`. A leaf that requires both an ML-DSA-44 and a
+Schnorr signature (template S2 of [p2mr-mldsa44.md](p2mr-mldsa44.md) in a leaf of version `0xe0`)
+cannot be spent at all once the checks are disabled.
+
+The rules are a soft fork: for the same parameters and the same parent, a block valid under them
+is valid without them.
+
+## Activation
+
+The rules are a buried deployment, `p2mr_tripwire` in `getdeploymentinfo`. They require P2MR.
+Wherever there are tripwire outputs, the height of the rules is above the height of
+`OP_CHECKMLDSA44`, so that the ML-DSA-44 leaf of a hybrid output (see [Wallets](#wallets)) is
+checked by the time its other leaf can fail.
+
+| Network | Height | Tripwire outputs |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Ark-0 signet | `ARK0_TRIPWIRE_HEIGHT` in `src/kernel/chainparams.h` | `ARK0_TRIPWIRE_OUTPOINTS` in `src/kernel/chainparams.cpp` |
+| other custom signets | `-signettripwire` | `-signettripwire` |
+| default signet, testnet3, testnet4, mainnet | never | none |
+| regtest | 0, or `-testactivationheight=p2mr_tripwire@<height>`, or `-signettripwire` | none, or `-signettripwire` |
+
+On the Ark-0 signet (challenge
+`5121026fc5d8e79a9fbc8bc3dea07d82641d16717ce0b000c13c512d8e8c1788c6e5da51ae`) the height and the
+outputs are rules of that network, fixed in the code; there is no option to change them, and once
+a build that carries them has been published they do not change. **This build does not set them
+yet**: `ARK0_TRIPWIRE_HEIGHT` is `std::numeric_limits<int>::max()` and the list is empty, so the
+rules never apply there. The commit that sets them adds both outputs, created below the height.
+
+Other custom signets, the networks for drills, take two options, which require `-signetchallenge`
+and which the Ark-0 signet refuses:
+
+    -signetmldsa44=<height>
+    -signettripwire=<height>@<txid:vout>[,<txid:vout>...]
+
+`-signetmldsa44` activates `OP_CHECKMLDSA44` from `<height>` (on other custom signets it never
+activates otherwise). `-signettripwire` activates these rules from `<height>` with the outpoints
+given as tripwire outputs; it requires `-signetmldsa44` with a lower height. A node refuses to start
+otherwise. Every node of such a network must use the same values: nodes with different ones
+follow different chains. On regtest `-signettripwire` takes the same form, requires the
+`p2mr_mldsa44` height to be lower, and cannot be combined with
+`-testactivationheight=p2mr_tripwire`. Mainnet and the testnets refuse both options at startup,
+whatever their value, and regtest refuses `-signetmldsa44`, rather than ignore them.
+
+At startup a node logs the rules and their state, for operators to compare:
+
+    Signet P2MR tripwire rules from height <height> in leaf version 0xe0; tripwire outputs <txid:vout> (BIP-341 NUMS) and <txid:vout> (drill key SHA256 <hash>)
+    P2MR tripwire state at height <tip>: tripwire outputs <txid:vout> unspent, <txid:vout> missing; elliptic curve checks in leaf version 0xe0 are disabled for the next block
+
+The labels and key hashes are only known for the outputs the code fixes; for outpoints given by
+an option only the outpoints are logged. On regtest the first line has no `Signet` prefix.
+
+A node without the rules keeps following the chain. A node that upgrades late reads the right
+state from its UTXO set, but does not check again the blocks it accepted before, like any soft
+fork: if one of them broke the rules, the late node stays on a chain that a node with the rules
+from the start rejects.
+
+## Policy
+
+- **Before activation**: while the next block is below the height, the mempool refuses spends
+  through leaves of version `0xe0` at depth one or more, as spends of an unknown leaf version
+  (`Taproot version reserved for soft-fork upgrades`), also with `-acceptnonstdtxn`.
+- **Standardness**: at every height, leaves of version `0xe0` follow the rules of leaves of version
+  `0xc0`: at most 80 bytes per witness element, the exemption of the signature chunks of templates
+  S1 and S2 at their exact sizes, and no other leaf using `OP_CHECKMLDSA44` at depth one or more.
+  Below the height this is stricter than a node without these rules for a spend at depth zero,
+  which nothing executes, with a witness element above 80 bytes: such a node takes it, this one
+  does not. That is policy only, the blocks either accepts are the same, and no transaction in
+  the mempool has to be checked again when the chain reaches the height.
+- **The mempool follows the next block**: script checks of the mempool add
+  `SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_EC_DISABLED` when the confirmed UTXO set leaves a tripwire output missing for
+  the next block. A tripwire spend that is only in the mempool changes nothing. A refused spend
+  reads `mempool-script-verify-flag-failed (Elliptic curve signature checks are disabled in this leaf)`.
+- **When the checks become disabled**: after a block that disables them is connected and the
+  tip updated, every mempool transaction that spends through a leaf of version `0xe0` at depth one
+  or more is checked again with the flags of the next block, its inputs read through the mempool.
+  Those that fail are removed with their descendants; spends through ML-DSA-44 leaves stay. During
+  a reorganization this happens once the transactions of the disconnected blocks are back in the
+  mempool, whenever the checks are disabled for the next block of the new tip, whatever they were
+  before. Each transaction of a package or a TRUC pair is checked on its own; descendants go with
+  their ancestors.
+- **Reorganizations below the height**: when the next block is below the height again, spends
+  through leaves of version `0xe0` at depth one or more are removed, with their descendants.
+- **The other way round**: when a reorganization brings the tripwire outputs back, nothing is
+  removed, and transactions removed earlier are not added back.
+
+## RPC and logs
+
+`gettripwireinfo` returns, from one snapshot of the active chain:
+
+- `tip_hash` and `tip_height`: the tip the state is read at;
+- `height` and `leaf_version` (`"e0"`): the rules;
+- `tripwires`: for each output, `outpoint` and `state`, which is `unspent` (present) or `missing`
+  (spent, never created, or created at or above the height: the UTXO set does not tell them
+  apart). On the Ark-0 signet the outputs the code fixes also have `key` (the x-only key of the
+  leaf) and `label` (`BIP-341 NUMS` or `drill`);
+- `ec_disabled_next_block`: whether the checks are disabled for the next block.
+
+It fails on networks where the rules are not enabled. It does not tell which transaction spent a
+tripwire output; a block explorer can find it in its own index.
+
+The node logs a spend of a tripwire output once the block that spends it is part of the active
+chain, and only there: not for proposals, candidate blocks that turn out invalid, blocks `VerifyDB`
+connects again, or the background chainstate of an assumeutxo snapshot.
+
+    P2MR tripwire <txid:vout> spent by <txid> in block <hash> (height <N>): elliptic curve checks in leaf version 0xe0 are disabled from height <max(height of the rules, N + 1)>
+    P2MR tripwire <txid:vout> spent by <txid> in block <hash> (height <N>): elliptic curve checks in leaf version 0xe0 are already disabled
+
+When the active chain reaches the height of the rules, the node logs their state:
+
+    P2MR tripwire rules active from height <height>, reached by block <hash>: tripwire outputs <txid:vout> unspent, ...; elliptic curve checks in leaf version 0xe0 are enabled for the next block
+
+## Wallets
+
+Bitcoin Core's wallet does not create or sign leaves of version `0xe0`. A wallet that does should:
+
+- use one template, S3: a tree of exactly two leaves, both of version `0xe0` and both at depth one,
+  one `<x-only key> OP_CHECKSIG` and one template S1 of [p2mr-mldsa44.md](p2mr-mldsa44.md). Never a
+  single leaf (depth zero), and never both checks in one leaf;
+- hand out such addresses only once the tip it has observed is at least six blocks past the height
+  of the rules, and refuse when it cannot read the state. On transactions it builds to fund them,
+  set `nLockTime` to the height and the `nSequence` of at least one input below `0xffffffff`, so
+  that no reorganization can confirm them below the height;
+- count an output as constrained only when its tree is exactly S3 and it confirmed at or above the
+  height; one confirmed below the height is not protected;
+- spend through the Schnorr leaf while `ec_disabled_next_block` is false, and through the
+  ML-DSA-44 leaf once it is true. Read the state again before broadcasting, and after a rejection or
+  a spend that left the mempool; decide by the state, not by the text of the rejection.
+
+## Tests
+
+- `src/test/data/script_tests.json`, block `P2MR leaf version 0xe0 tests`, written by
+  `test/util/generate_p2mr_tripwire_script_tests.py`: each combination of
+  `P2MR_TRIPWIRE` and `P2MR_EC_DISABLED`, the order of the checks, depth zero, the annex, the
+  control byte, signatures across leaf versions, and leaves of other versions, P2TR, legacy and
+  witness v0 scripts under `P2MR_EC_DISABLED`.
+- `src/test/data/script_tests.json`, the block above it, `P2MR tripwired leaf (version 0xe0) rows
+  signed by p2mr-ts`: rows signed by another implementation, the TypeScript library
+  [p2mr-ts](https://github.com/duncan0k/p2mr-ts), whose `scripts/generate-tripwire-crossimpl.ts`
+  writes them; the generator above leaves them in place.
+- `src/test/p2mr_tripwire_tests.cpp`: the parameters of each network, the options and the state,
+  and that `ConnectBlock` decides on the view it is given, which the background chainstate of an
+  assumeutxo snapshot relies on and which the functional test cannot show: the regtest snapshot
+  it loads has no spend of a leaf of version `0xe0` in its history.
+- `test/functional/feature_p2mr_tripwire.py`: activation, the trigger, the mempool, reorganizations,
+  the caches, restarts, pruning, assumeutxo, a late upgrade, the logs and the RPC.
